WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.391
Hi there, how's it going? This is a

2
00:00:02.391 --> 00:00:04.381
short demo of our

3
00:00:04.381 --> 00:00:05.803
distributed system, which I built

4
00:00:05.803 --> 00:00:08.321
with engineer Matías Bartellone. This

5
00:00:08.321 --> 00:00:10.823
is a dashboard showing the live state

6
00:00:10.823 --> 00:00:12.700
of our distributed pipeline, which

7
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.951
we use to analyse

8
00:00:13.951 --> 00:00:16.141
money-laundering patterns. Given a

9
00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:18.330
pile of transactions — a gigantic

10
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.207
transaction dataset — what it does is

11
00:00:20.207 --> 00:00:21.145
detect the fraudulent

12
00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:22.709
transactions, and also return which

13
00:00:22.709 --> 00:00:24.899
accounts are involved in

14
00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:26.150
all those fraudulent transactions.

15
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.844
Let's start by running a medium-sized

16
00:00:30.844 --> 00:00:33.826
dataset, HI-Medium, three

17
00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:35.803
gigabytes, to show how it looks

18
00:00:35.803 --> 00:00:37.122
visually, while I keep

19
00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:38.769
explaining how it works. We

20
00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:40.746
designed it as a layered architecture

21
00:00:40.746 --> 00:00:42.394
focused mainly on

22
00:00:42.394 --> 00:00:44.041
optimising distribution. Here we see how

23
00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:47.007
the data starts coming in and moving

24
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:49.540
through the pipeline. So,

25
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.484
we built a layered architecture,

26
00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.428
focused on optimising load

27
00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:55.372
distribution and guaranteeing the least

28
00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:56.668
reprocessing possible. All the

29
00:00:56.668 --> 00:00:58.288
communication and routing — that is,

30
00:00:58.288 --> 00:01:00.232
how the containers and the different

31
00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:02.176
entities talk to each other — is

32
00:01:02.176 --> 00:01:03.796
done through queues and exchanges,

33
00:01:03.796 --> 00:01:05.416
all implemented with RabbitMQ.

34
00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:08.008
This is what lets us scale

35
00:01:08.008 --> 00:01:09.629
each stage horizontally and

36
00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:11.573
independently. So there's no stage

37
00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:14.417
that becomes a bottleneck, because if

38
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:17.154
one stage is slower than the

39
00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:19.283
rest and everyone is waiting for that

40
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:21.413
stage to finish, that stage can be scaled:

41
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:23.238
you scale it, it speeds up, and then

42
00:01:23.238 --> 00:01:24.758
nobody is ever waiting. They all

43
00:01:24.758 --> 00:01:26.279
run steadily,

44
00:01:26.279 --> 00:01:28.701
consistently and in parallel. Now,

45
00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.459
it isn't just scalable and fast —

46
00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:32.509
we also assume the nodes

47
00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.560
are going to fail. So we made it

48
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.317
fault-tolerant, to guarantee

49
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:38.075
high availability. What we did was

50
00:01:38.075 --> 00:01:39.832
implement a medic service, which

51
00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.883
we see here in the corner. This

52
00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:44.226
medic service is a cluster of medics,

53
00:01:44.226 --> 00:01:46.276
which, using a bully algorithm,

54
00:01:46.276 --> 00:01:48.620
elect a leader. The idea is that this

55
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.256
leader is in charge of sending

56
00:01:51.256 --> 00:01:54.242
heartbeats to the nodes, and if

57
00:01:54.242 --> 00:01:56.256
a node doesn't answer, it assumes

58
00:01:56.256 --> 00:01:58.270
it's down or dead, and as a medic it

59
00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.837
takes action to bring it back

60
00:01:59.837 --> 00:02:01.627
up and revive it. Why more than one

61
00:02:01.627 --> 00:02:03.417
leader — more than one medic, I mean? Because if

62
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:05.655
there were only one, and the medic

63
00:02:05.655 --> 00:02:07.222
dies, there's nobody left to resurrect

64
00:02:07.222 --> 00:02:08.789
the other nodes that go down. So we have

65
00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.027
more than one medic, and there's no single point

66
00:02:11.027 --> 00:02:13.041
of failure. If one medic goes down, the others

67
00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:14.831
still up can take over as leader

68
00:02:14.831 --> 00:02:15.950
through that bully algorithm, to

69
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.150
decide who the leader will be and

70
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.958
take over its job until it

71
00:02:19.958 --> 00:02:22.671
comes back up. In this

72
00:02:22.671 --> 00:02:24.742
panel we also built

73
00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:27.108
chaos here. What it does is

74
00:02:27.108 --> 00:02:29.414
start killing nodes

75
00:02:29.414 --> 00:02:31.138
dynamically and at random, so the medic

76
00:02:31.138 --> 00:02:32.645
brings them back up. I've just

77
00:02:32.645 --> 00:02:35.214
switched it on. And then, in terms of

78
00:02:35.214 --> 00:02:36.450
performance, what results

79
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.048
did we get? We tested it

80
00:02:39.048 --> 00:02:40.782
on hardware where we only had

81
00:02:40.782 --> 00:02:43.384
6 cores and 12 threads available,

82
00:02:43.384 --> 00:02:45.408
which is extremely little. And in the

83
00:02:45.408 --> 00:02:48.403
tests we ran, the maximum load

84
00:02:48.403 --> 00:02:51.175
we pushed it to was 16 gigabytes, which

85
00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:52.562
comes to 180 million financial

86
00:02:52.562 --> 00:02:54.988
transactions, and it can process that in under

87
00:02:54.988 --> 00:02:57.760
4 minutes. That's 240 gigabytes

88
00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.533
per hour, 5 terabytes per day, which

89
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.305
is 170 terabytes per month — far

90
00:03:03.305 --> 00:03:06.078
more volume than what some companies

91
00:03:06.078 --> 00:03:08.157
in the industry are handling

92
00:03:08.157 --> 00:03:10.858
today. Just now you could see

93
00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:12.636
one of them go down.

94
00:03:12.636 --> 00:03:14.414
It even went into recovery and came

95
00:03:14.414 --> 00:03:16.192
back up. It's red when it's

96
00:03:16.192 --> 00:03:17.970
down, yellow when it's coming

97
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.748
back up, and green when it's

98
00:03:19.748 --> 00:03:21.780
working. Look here — I'll come back in 3

99
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.286
seconds. This zero will turn

100
00:03:24.286 --> 00:03:26.090
green again and start working,

101
00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.442
picking its usual processing back up. And

102
00:03:27.442 --> 00:03:28.795
there it recovered completely. Well, that's

103
00:03:28.795 --> 00:03:30.599
everything for the demo. Thanks

104
00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:31.050
for watching.

